      "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{-x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}-{u}_{2}{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}-{u}_{2}{e}^{-x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}-{u}_{2}{e}^{-x} & =2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}-{u}_{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}-\\cancel{{u}_{2}{e}^{-x}} & =\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{{u}_{2}{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}=0.\n$$",
